  "title": "ASP.NET Core authentication principals claims roles authorization policies and handlers",
  "status": "source-backed-draft-generation-enabled",
  "inventoryComplete": true,
  "concepts": [
    "Authentication establishes a ClaimsPrincipal for the current request; authorization evaluates whether that principal satisfies requirements for an endpoint or a specific resource. Successful authentication alone never implies permission to perform every action.",
    "A scheme names an authentication handler and its options. Authenticate, challenge, and forbid are different operations: authenticate builds a principal, challenge asks an unauthenticated caller to authenticate, and forbid reports that an authenticated principal is not allowed.",
    "Authorization policies are sets of requirements. Requirements within a policy must all succeed, while multiple handlers can cooperate to satisfy a requirement. A handler may leave a requirement pending, succeed it, or fail the whole context explicitly.",
    "The default policy applies when authorization is explicitly requested without a named policy; the fallback policy applies only when no authorization metadata produced a policy. Resource-based decisions happen after loading the resource and must be checked against that actual resource, not inferred from a URL or role name."
  ],
